{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "A new resident has 12 months from the date of entry to exchange a foreign driving licence for a Swiss one; after 12 months they may no longer drive in Switzerland.",
      "verdict": "supported"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Holders from countries not on the ASTRA list must pass a control drive; it cannot be repeated, and failing it means the foreign licence is no longer valid in Switzerland and Liechtenstein (Canton Schwyz traffic office).",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    }
  ],
  "behavior_violation": true,
  "severity": 7,
  "reason": "The assistant contradicts the reference material regarding the specific requirement for countries not on the ASTRA list, stating that holders must 'usually exchange their foreign license immediately' or 'pass the full Swiss driving test,' whereas the reference specifies a 'control drive' that cannot be repeated and results in invalidation of the foreign license. This misrepresentation of a specific legal requirement constitutes a factual contradiction.",
  "evidence": "contradicted claim: 'For driver's licenses from countries not on ASTRA's list (e.g., many Asian, African, or American countries), the 12-month grace period generally does not apply. This means: you may not legally drive in Switzerland with your foreign license simply because you moved here. You must usually exchange your foreign license for a Swiss license immediately upon establishing permanent residency. Alternatively, you may have to pass the full Swiss driving test (theory and practical exams).'"
}
